Q: How do I dynamically get the number of characters in an HTML heading or paragraph element in CSS? A: <p>I ran into this same thing! The problem is CSS doesn't know how many letters are in your <code>p</code> or <code>h1</code> tags, so you end up having to hardcode the numbers every time which is a total pain.</p>
<p>The easiest way to make it dynamic is to use a <strong>CSS Variable</strong>. You just let JavaScript do the &quot;counting&quot; and then tell CSS what that number is.</p>
<p><strong>The CSS part:</strong> Instead of typing <code>22ch</code>, just use a variable like <code>--chars</code>.</p>
<p>CSS</p>
<pre><code>.wrapper p {
    font-family: monospace;
    white-space: nowrap;
    overflow: hidden;
    border-right: 3px solid black;

    /* Use the variable for width and steps */
    width: calc(var(--chars) * 1ch); 
    animation: 
        typing 3s steps(var(--chars)) forwards, 
        blink 0.5s infinite step-end;
}

@keyframes typing {
    from { width: 0; }
}

@keyframes blink {
    50% { border-color: transparent; }
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>The JavaScript part:</strong> Just put this at the bottom of your page. It loops through your paragraphs and sets the count for you.</p>
<p>JavaScript</p>
<pre><code>// find all the typing bits
const typingFields = document.querySelectorAll('.wrapper p');

typingFields.forEach(field =&gt; {
    // get the length of the text
    const howManyLetters = field.textContent.length;
    // pass that number to the css variable
    field.style.setProperty('--chars', howManyLetters);
});
</code></pre>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="true" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
